<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="diagnostics-policy" title="AW3xxx - policy and plugins">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Two unrelated conditions share this range. One is a class the engine refuses to weave.
        The other is a <code>WeaverPlugin</code> that misbehaved. The chapters split on that, so
        read one and skip the other. <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic codes</a> covers the
        output line itself.
    </p>

    <chapter title="A class the engine refused" id="policy">

        <p>
            <code>AW3001</code>, <code>AW3002</code> and <code>AW3003</code> share one gate. It
            runs per class, right before rewriting. A refused class is handed back exactly as it
            arrived. <code>AW3010</code> and <code>AW3020</code> are not from it.
            <code>AW3010</code> fires per classpath entry while manifests are read.
            <code>AW3020</code> fires once a signed jar is woven.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3001</code></td>
                <td>The target sits under a JDK prefix</td>
                <td>Weave a class of your own instead</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3002</code></td>
                <td>The target came from a signed jar</td>
                <td>Weave before signing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3003</code></td>
                <td>The target is %product% itself</td>
                <td>No remedy; no setting reopens it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3010</code></td>
                <td>A weave arrived transitively</td>
                <td>Declare that dependency directly</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3020</code></td>
                <td>A signed jar was woven anyway</td>
                <td>Nothing to do; check it was meant</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Four of these nineteen are warnings: <code>AW3010</code>, <code>AW3020</code>,
            <code>AW3118</code> and <code>AW3120</code>. The rest are errors, and a build stops
            at one unless the driver says otherwise. <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic
            codes</a> has the driver-by-driver rule.
        </p>

        <p>
            <code>java.*</code> is refused whatever you configure. Those classes load before any
            transformer exists. The two overrides, and what each costs, are in
            <a href="policy-and-safety.topic">Policy and safety</a>.
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> covers where a weave comes
            from.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Reopening any other JDK prefix">
                <p>
                    Takes a hand-built policy. The key the message names reaches no driver.
                    <code>DefaultWeavePolicy.builder().allowPackage(...)</code> is what works.
                    <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has the keys the drivers do
                    read.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Only two places, in practice">
                <p>
                    <code>AW3002</code> is raised in three places, but the engine's own gate
                    never builds a target its third can refuse. Only the weaving class loader
                    and the <code>weave</code> goal weaving dependency jars reach a reader, both
                    with an override. <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a>
                    compares the three drivers.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Once per signed jar">
                <p>
                    <code>AW3020</code> fires once for every signed jar the <code>weave</code>
                    goal considers, before any of its classes is read, and only when
                    <code>weaveDependencies</code> is set. There is nothing to do about it beyond
                    confirming the decision.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Under weave-tests">
                <p>
                    Your own main classes sit outside the direct set, so a weave declared there
                    is reported as <code>AW3010</code> against your module. Neither offered
                    remedy fits, and there is nothing to do about it: a module cannot depend on
                    itself. <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the goal.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="While a plugin loads" id="loading">

        <p>
            No shipped driver loads a <code>WeaverPlugin</code>. Maven, the agent and the
            weaving class loader never call <code>discoverPlugins</code> or <code>plugin</code>,
            so <code>CorePlugin</code> is the only one any of them runs. These codes reach a
            plugin author only through a <code>Weaver</code> built by hand.
        </p>

        <p>
            Every candidate then passes four gates in order; the first that refuses drops that
            plugin alone. Its numbering interleaves with the next chapter's — a code missing here
            is there instead. <a href="loading-plugins.topic">Loading and isolating plugins</a> has the
            order, and <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a> is the section.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3101</code></td>
                <td>It claims the built-in namespace</td>
                <td>Give the plugin its own namespace</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3111</code></td>
                <td>Two contributors claim one name</td>
                <td>Remove a jar, or drop one registration</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3112</code></td>
                <td>It needs a newer SPI level</td>
                <td>Upgrade %product%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3113</code></td>
                <td>It declared an SPI level below 1</td>
                <td>Return <code>WeaverApi.LEVEL</code> from <code>apiLevel()</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3114</code></td>
                <td>Construction, <code>id()</code> or <code>apiLevel()</code> threw</td>
                <td>Check the constructor and both methods</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3119</code></td>
                <td>The configuration refuses it</td>
                <td>Add the namespace to allow, clear deny</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>Three settings decide what may load, and they do not weigh equally:</p>

        <list>
            <li><p><code>aether.weaver.plugins.enabled=false</code> refuses every plugin, and
                neither list is consulted.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>aether.weaver.plugins.deny</code> wins over the allowlist, so a denied
                namespace stays denied.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>aether.weaver.plugins.allow</code> is exhaustive once it names anything;
                empty means unconfigured.</p></li>
        </list>

        <note>
            <p>
                A driver has to read these three keys itself and pass a predicate to
                <code>WeaverBuilder.permitPlugins(...)</code>, typically built with
                <code>PluginFilter.from(enabled, allow, deny)</code>. No shipped driver does, so
                writing any of the three today changes nothing. The <code>AW3119</code> remedy
                names the allowlist and only the allowlist, so following it does nothing when the
                refusal came from the other two. <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has
                the keys a driver could read.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="While a plugin contributes and runs" id="running">

        <p>
            A throw out of a <code>WeaverPlugin</code>'s code becomes a diagnostic, but not
            always against the plugin that threw. <code>AW3116</code> stays narrow — a built-in
            point is called directly, so a defect in the engine surfaces as itself.
            <code>AW3117</code> does not: it guards the whole class rewrite. A built-in defect on
            a class a plugin also touches is reported as <code>AW3117</code> against that plugin
            instead. Report rather than throw is the rule behind both.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3100</code></td>
                <td>Its namespace failed validation</td>
                <td>Reachable only outside plugin loading</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3110</code></td>
                <td>It registered outside its namespace</td>
                <td>Name it with the plugin's namespace</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3115</code></td>
                <td>It threw while registering</td>
                <td>Report through the context instead</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3116</code></td>
                <td>A contributed point threw</td>
                <td>Return an empty result and report</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3117</code></td>
                <td>A contributed injector threw</td>
                <td>Make emission total; nothing was written</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3118</code></td>
                <td>An observer threw</td>
                <td>Check the observer; weaving went on</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3120</code></td>
                <td>A retired identifier was used</td>
                <td>Use the replacement the message names</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW3121</code></td>
                <td>An alias names nothing registered</td>
                <td>Register the replacement, or fix it</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <code>AW3100</code> tests a namespace inside <code>NamespacedRegistry.Builder</code>
            itself. A plugin's own bad namespace never reaches it; <code>PluginId</code>'s
            constructor refuses it first. This fires only for a caller building a registry by
            hand.
        </p>

        <p>
            An identifier belongs to whoever registered it, which is why most of these name a
            namespace. <code>AW3120</code> and <code>AW3121</code> do not: they quote the alias
            as written, and an alias between two unqualified identifiers names no namespace at
            all. <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the shape.
            <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> covers what a contributed
            point resolves against.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                The engine resolves every identifier through a listener that discards what it is
                given, so no build prints <code>AW3120</code>. Check a plugin's own release notes
                for a retired identifier instead.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

